1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,620
In 1993, a game called Doom ran on a
computer with four megabytes of memory.

2
00:00:06,261 --> 00:00:09,421
That's about the size
of a single photo on your phone.

3
00:00:10,091 --> 00:00:12,171
There was no 3D accelerator.

4
00:00:12,951 --> 00:00:15,071
The main processor did all the rendering.

5
00:00:15,863 --> 00:00:19,023
Today, a developer starts with an engine,
like Unity or Unreal,

6
00:00:19,253 --> 00:00:23,463
that takes care of graphics, sound,
memory and the hardware.

7
00:00:24,173 --> 00:00:26,713
On DOS, all of that
was the developer's problem.

8
00:00:26,793 --> 00:00:29,293
And quite often, the player's too.

9
00:00:29,945 --> 00:00:32,565
So how hard was it to make a game for DOS?

10
00:00:35,205 --> 00:00:38,265
Let's start with memory,
because memory was a mess.

11
00:00:38,907 --> 00:00:44,727
The original IBM PC, from 1981,
could address one megabyte of memory.

12
00:00:45,024 --> 00:00:49,124
But only the first six hundred and forty
kilobytes were meant for programs.

13
00:00:49,877 --> 00:00:53,717
The other three hundred and eighty-four
were reserved for video memory,

14
00:00:53,797 --> 00:00:55,717
expansion cards and the BIOS.

15
00:00:56,587 --> 00:01:01,447
For more than a decade, DOS normally
started programs inside that space.

16
00:01:02,187 --> 00:01:04,467
Anything beyond it took extra work.

17
00:01:05,647 --> 00:01:07,727
That's where the famous line comes from.

18
00:01:07,807 --> 00:01:11,067
640K ought to be enough for anybody.

19
00:01:11,847 --> 00:01:13,967
It's usually credited to Bill Gates.

20
00:01:14,687 --> 00:01:18,067
There's no record that he ever said it,
and he denies it.

21
00:01:19,137 --> 00:01:23,217
By the early nineties,
a PC came with four megabytes, or eight.

22
00:01:23,541 --> 00:01:26,881
And a game could still stop
with the words "not enough memory".

23
00:01:27,605 --> 00:01:30,965
Having the memory and being able to use it
were two different things.

24
00:01:33,395 --> 00:01:36,855
One way around it was expanded memory,
or EMS.

25
00:01:37,757 --> 00:01:42,017
A sixty-four kilobyte window sat
in the upper part of that first megabyte.

26
00:01:42,417 --> 00:01:48,217
Through it, a program could see four pages
of extra memory, sixteen kilobytes each,

27
00:01:48,449 --> 00:01:51,709
and swap them for others,
like turning the pages of a book

28
00:01:51,939 --> 00:01:53,769
through a hole in a sheet of paper.

29
00:01:54,508 --> 00:01:58,488
Another way was extended memory,
reached through a driver called HIMEM.

30
00:02:00,804 --> 00:02:03,884
And this is where the developer's problem
became the player's.

31
00:02:04,719 --> 00:02:07,979
Drivers for the mouse, the CD drive
or the sound card

32
00:02:08,209 --> 00:02:12,639
took a bite out of that precious memory,
unless you managed to load them high.

33
00:02:13,005 --> 00:02:16,805
So players learned to edit two files,
CONFIG.SYS and AUTOEXEC.BAT.

34
00:02:18,435 --> 00:02:20,135
One game wanted EMS.

35
00:02:21,255 --> 00:02:24,765
Another wanted it switched off,
with a setting called NOEMS.

36
00:02:25,135 --> 00:02:28,195
And a third needed every free kilobyte.

37
00:02:28,625 --> 00:02:33,025
In 1993, MS-DOS 6 added a menu at startup.

38
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:37,080
You picked the configuration
for the game you wanted to play,

39
00:02:37,240 --> 00:02:39,640
and the computer started up for that game.

40
00:02:40,252 --> 00:02:44,512
Some people ended up with a separate entry
for almost every game they owned.

41
00:02:46,932 --> 00:02:50,932
But by then, the bigger games had found
a way around the limits of DOS.

42
00:02:51,443 --> 00:02:54,523
Since the 386,
the processor itself could run

43
00:02:54,753 --> 00:02:59,708
in 32-bit protected mode,
with room for up to four gigabytes.

44
00:03:00,008 --> 00:03:02,688
We've looked at protected mode
in an earlier video.

45
00:03:02,768 --> 00:03:05,348
DOS, though, couldn't use it.

46
00:03:05,587 --> 00:03:09,067
So games brought their own helper,
a DOS extender.

47
00:03:09,787 --> 00:03:11,107
The best known was DOS/4GW.

48
00:03:13,237 --> 00:03:16,916
It came bundled with a popular compiler,
Watcom C.

49
00:03:17,016 --> 00:03:20,656
When a game started,
DOS/4GW switched the processor

50
00:03:20,886 --> 00:03:23,736
into protected mode,
giving the game access

51
00:03:23,966 --> 00:03:26,176
to memory beyond the DOS limit.

52
00:03:26,840 --> 00:03:31,460
DOS was still there underneath,
providing services like file access.

53
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:37,160
But the game now ran in protected mode,
not as a traditional DOS program.

54
00:03:37,882 --> 00:03:40,322
If you played DOS games in the nineties,

55
00:03:40,402 --> 00:03:42,802
you've seen its name
flash by on the screen.

56
00:03:45,572 --> 00:03:46,832
Now, the picture.

57
00:03:47,792 --> 00:03:50,452
In one of the most widely used VGA modes,

58
00:03:50,532 --> 00:03:53,662
called Mode 13h,
the screen was three hundred and twenty

59
00:03:53,892 --> 00:03:58,405
by two hundred pixels,
in two hundred and fifty-six colours.

60
00:03:58,705 --> 00:04:00,425
Each pixel was one byte.

61
00:04:00,985 --> 00:04:03,605
To colour a pixel,
you wrote a number into memory.

62
00:04:04,105 --> 00:04:07,085
No graphics library. Just memory.

63
00:04:07,829 --> 00:04:10,649
The whole screen took
sixty-four thousand bytes.

64
00:04:11,403 --> 00:04:15,523
That's just under sixty-four kilobytes,
small enough to fit in one segment,

65
00:04:15,723 --> 00:04:19,123
the block a 16-bit program
could address in one go.

66
00:04:20,023 --> 00:04:22,343
For once, memory was on your side.

67
00:04:23,514 --> 00:04:25,954
But if you draw straight
onto the visible screen,

68
00:04:26,034 --> 00:04:29,714
the player sees half-finished pictures,
and the image flickers.

69
00:04:30,115 --> 00:04:32,915
So games drew each frame
in a hidden buffer first,

70
00:04:32,995 --> 00:04:35,815
then copied the finished image
into video memory.

71
00:04:38,405 --> 00:04:43,425
In 1991, Michael Abrash, a programmer
and writer, published a better way.

72
00:04:43,705 --> 00:04:45,405
He called it Mode X.

73
00:04:46,119 --> 00:04:50,179
It used a part of the VGA
that most programmers had never touched.

74
00:04:50,845 --> 00:04:55,025
It gave square pixels, and room
for several screens in video memory.

75
00:04:55,698 --> 00:04:59,698
The game drew on a screen nobody
could see, and then simply switched to it.

76
00:05:00,198 --> 00:05:02,158
But it was much harder to program.

77
00:05:04,788 --> 00:05:08,068
Then there was sound.
And sound was a lottery.

78
00:05:08,790 --> 00:05:11,690
The PC's own speaker was built to beep.

79
00:05:11,993 --> 00:05:15,113
Then came sound cards,
and other sound hardware.

80
00:05:15,733 --> 00:05:17,793
No two of them worked the same way.

81
00:05:18,341 --> 00:05:21,741
AdLib, with a Yamaha chip
that synthesised music.

82
00:05:22,061 --> 00:05:25,721
Sound Blaster, which could also play
recorded sound.

83
00:05:26,397 --> 00:05:30,917
The Roland MT-32,
an expensive box with richer instruments.

84
00:05:31,657 --> 00:05:32,857
The Gravis UltraSound.

85
00:05:33,961 --> 00:05:37,101
Supporting all of them
meant writing different drivers.

86
00:05:37,619 --> 00:05:40,119
And the game had to know
where the card was.

87
00:05:40,559 --> 00:05:43,639
Its port, its interrupt,
and its DMA channel.

88
00:05:44,266 --> 00:05:49,446
The usual answer, for a Sound Blaster,
was port 220, IRQ 7, DMA 1.

89
00:05:49,526 --> 00:05:51,366
Usually.

90
00:05:52,087 --> 00:05:55,867
Get one of them wrong,
and the game was silent. Or it froze.

91
00:05:57,286 --> 00:06:01,406
In 1991, a programmer called John Miles
released a library

92
00:06:01,636 --> 00:06:05,006
that talked to many sound cards
through a single interface.

93
00:06:05,702 --> 00:06:10,382
A game could write its sound code once,
and let the library deal with the card.

94
00:06:11,003 --> 00:06:13,583
It was another layer
between the game and the hardware.

95
00:06:14,223 --> 00:06:15,583
Keep that in mind.

96
00:06:18,093 --> 00:06:19,513
And then there was speed.

97
00:06:20,193 --> 00:06:23,293
Programmers counted
every step the processor took.

98
00:06:23,951 --> 00:06:25,871
To find a pixel on the screen,

99
00:06:25,991 --> 00:06:29,251
you multiply its row
by three hundred and twenty.

100
00:06:29,551 --> 00:06:33,031
On older processors,
multiplying could be expensive.

101
00:06:33,635 --> 00:06:38,105
But three hundred and twenty is two
hundred and fifty-six plus sixty-four.

102
00:06:38,455 --> 00:06:40,275
Both are powers of two.

103
00:06:40,459 --> 00:06:44,539
So you can replace the multiplication
with two bit shifts and an addition.

104
00:06:45,891 --> 00:06:48,551
Chris Sawyer went all
the way down to the metal.

105
00:06:49,351 --> 00:06:54,931
He wrote Transport Tycoon, in 1994,
almost entirely in assembly language,

106
00:06:55,067 --> 00:06:59,327
the instructions the processor
itself understands, one by one.

107
00:07:01,435 --> 00:07:04,335
And sometimes,
even all of that wasn't enough.

108
00:07:05,009 --> 00:07:07,529
In 1990, Wing Commander had a bug

109
00:07:07,759 --> 00:07:10,789
that the team couldn't find,
with the deadline close.

110
00:07:11,162 --> 00:07:14,942
When you quit the game, the memory
manager crashed with an error message.

111
00:07:15,374 --> 00:07:18,674
So a programmer, Ken Demarest,
opened the memory manager

112
00:07:18,904 --> 00:07:22,454
in a hex editor
and changed the text of that message.

113
00:07:23,129 --> 00:07:25,969
Now it said:
"Thank you for playing Wing Commander."

114
00:07:26,830 --> 00:07:30,350
Then the team found the real bug,
and the joke never shipped.

115
00:07:31,086 --> 00:07:33,546
Some players are still sure they saw it.

116
00:07:34,106 --> 00:07:37,626
And later Origin games
really did say goodbye like that.

117
00:07:40,176 --> 00:07:41,916
Which brings us back to Doom.

118
00:07:43,456 --> 00:07:48,276
Doom used DOS/4GW,
and ran as a 32-bit program.

119
00:07:49,035 --> 00:07:52,415
Floating-point hardware
wasn't guaranteed on every PC.

120
00:07:52,869 --> 00:07:55,149
So Doom used fixed-point arithmetic.

121
00:07:56,069 --> 00:07:59,609
Whole numbers, with the lower sixteen bits
standing for the fraction.

122
00:08:00,391 --> 00:08:02,311
And its world wasn't
really three-dimensional.

123
00:08:03,922 --> 00:08:08,402
Every level is a flat floor plan, with a
height for each floor and each ceiling.

124
00:08:09,031 --> 00:08:11,131
One room can never sit above another.

125
00:08:11,211 --> 00:08:14,011
And you can't properly look up or down.

126
00:08:14,655 --> 00:08:19,735
Before a level could be played, a tool
cut the map into a binary tree, a BSP.

127
00:08:20,468 --> 00:08:23,068
The engine walks that tree
from front to back,

128
00:08:23,188 --> 00:08:26,928
starting on the player's side,
and skips areas that can't be seen.

129
00:08:28,119 --> 00:08:30,579
And Doom wasn't even written on DOS.

130
00:08:31,415 --> 00:08:36,995
id Software built it on NeXT workstations,
and compiled the result for DOS.

131
00:08:37,804 --> 00:08:40,164
Doom wasn't a general 3D engine.

132
00:08:40,884 --> 00:08:43,384
It was built for exactly
one kind of world.

133
00:08:44,024 --> 00:08:47,044
That specialisation
helped it run on a 386.

134
00:08:47,859 --> 00:08:50,259
And other studios wanted that world.

135
00:08:50,963 --> 00:08:57,643
Raven Software licensed Doom's engine
for Heretic, in 1994, and Hexen, in 1995.

136
00:08:58,653 --> 00:09:01,853
Doom helped establish
a new way of making games.

137
00:09:02,393 --> 00:09:07,073
Studios could license an existing engine,
instead of building it all from scratch.

138
00:09:09,753 --> 00:09:14,193
A memory extender.
A sound library. A licensed engine.

139
00:09:14,823 --> 00:09:17,483
Each one took a problem
off the developer's hands.

140
00:09:17,843 --> 00:09:22,563
And the layers kept growing, into the
engines that developers start with today.

141
00:09:25,573 --> 00:09:28,013
So was making games harder back then?

142
00:09:28,333 --> 00:09:29,813
In many ways, yes.

143
00:09:30,356 --> 00:09:32,256
But the difficulty didn't disappear.

144
00:09:32,896 --> 00:09:34,096
It moved.

145
00:09:35,286 --> 00:09:37,706
Today, developers fight
with gigabytes of assets

146
00:09:37,936 --> 00:09:41,966
and millions of polygons,
instead of a few hundred kilobytes.

147
00:09:42,443 --> 00:09:45,943
On DOS, even the player
had to know where their memory went.

148
00:09:47,193 --> 00:09:49,033
So here's a question for you.

149
00:09:49,573 --> 00:09:52,793
Do you remember building
a boot menu just to get one game running?

150
00:09:53,413 --> 00:09:55,613
And was it a struggle, or half the fun?

151
00:09:56,053 --> 00:09:57,343
Let me know in the comments.
